@format tqd 1.0

!#
  Northwind contextual layer — the statement-level context QROS exists for.

  The base Northwind data is a relational export: every fact is flat and
  unqualified, and the only tags on it are import provenance (@source,
  @importedAt). That is enough to make tag SYNTAX run, and not enough to
  demonstrate anything — a corpus whose tags only say "which file this row
  came from" cannot show why per-statement context matters.

  This layer adds two things the base data cannot express, using predicates of
  its own so the base corpus counts are untouched (the rule canon's 2155 / 228
  / 69 and the README's 77 products / 830 orders all range over `:unitPrice`,
  `:quantity`, `:discount`, `:hasProduct` and `:discontinued`, none of which
  appear here):

  1. A PRICE HISTORY on `:listPrice`, bounded with @validFrom / @validUntil.
     `AS OF` now DISCRIMINATES: the same query at two dates returns two
     different prices. Against the base data `AS OF` runs and can only ever
     return everything, which proves nothing.

  2. DISAGREEING STOCK READINGS on `:stockReading` — the same subject and
     predicate, different values, told apart only by their tags (@source,
     @confidence, @authority, @measuredAt). This is Law 1: a statement's
     identity includes its context, so these are distinct facts rather than a
     contradiction to be resolved on ingest. It gives tag filters something
     that CHANGES THE ANSWER: filter to the audited reading and you get one
     number, filter to the supplier's and you get another.

  Everything here is invented for the corpus and says so. Northwind has no
  real price history; inventing one is legitimate for a test corpus, and
  pretending it is authentic would not be.
#!

@layer instance {

    # ---- Price history: three products, each with a superseded and a current
    # price. The superseded rows carry @validUntil, the current rows do not,
    # which is what makes an as-of query at an earlier date return a different
    # answer than one at today's date.

    :product-38 :listPrice "210.00" @validFrom=1996-01-01 @validUntil=1996-12-31 @source=price-list-1996 @authority=finance .
    :product-38 :listPrice "263.50" @validFrom=1997-01-01 @source=price-list-1997 @authority=finance .

    :product-29 :listPrice "99.00"  @validFrom=1996-01-01 @validUntil=1996-12-31 @source=price-list-1996 @authority=finance .
    :product-29 :listPrice "123.79" @validFrom=1997-01-01 @source=price-list-1997 @authority=finance .

    :product-59 :listPrice "50.00"  @validFrom=1996-01-01 @validUntil=1996-12-31 @source=price-list-1996 @authority=finance .
    :product-59 :listPrice "55.00"  @validFrom=1997-01-01 @source=price-list-1997 @authority=finance .

    # ---- Disagreeing stock readings. Same subject, same predicate, different
    # values — distinguished ONLY by their tags. An audit and a supplier feed
    # disagree about the same shelf, and both statements are true within their
    # own context.

    :product-38 :stockReading "17" @source=warehouse-audit  @confidence=0.98 @authority=operations @measuredAt=1997-05-01 .
    :product-38 :stockReading "22" @source=supplier-feed    @confidence=0.60 @authority=supplier   @measuredAt=1997-05-01 .

    :product-29 :stockReading "0"  @source=warehouse-audit  @confidence=0.98 @authority=operations @measuredAt=1997-05-01 .
    :product-29 :stockReading "4"  @source=supplier-feed    @confidence=0.55 @authority=supplier   @measuredAt=1997-05-01 .

    :product-59 :stockReading "31" @source=warehouse-audit  @confidence=0.95 @authority=operations @measuredAt=1997-05-01 .

    # A reading with no confidence at all — so @confidence NOT_EXISTS matches
    # something, rather than being a filter that can never fire.
    :product-60 :stockReading "12" @source=floor-count @authority=operations @measuredAt=1997-05-02 .
}
